mcs51 peephole: ignore // comments when scanning for the next rule

readRules() scans raw text for the tokens "replace" and "barrier" to
find the start of the next rule.  A comment between rules that contains
either word (e.g. "// this barrier is needed because ...") confuses the
scanner: it can set the barrier flag spuriously or treat the comment as
the start of a rule.

Real-world peephole files use // comments between rules, so skip the
rest of the line when // is seen during the token scan.

Part of a series splitting a larger mcs51 peephole set into independent
pieces.

--- SDCCpeeph.c	2026-03-11 09:20:48.000000000 +0100
+++ SDCCpeeph.c	2026-03-12 17:00:00.000000000 +0100
@@ -2945,6 +2945,9 @@
      start of a rule */
   while (*bp && strncmp (bp, "replace", 7))
     {
+      /* ignore text in // comments */
+      if (bp[0] == '/' && bp[1] == '/')
+        { while (*bp && *bp != '\n') bp++; continue; }
       if (!strncmp (bp, "barrier", 7))
         barrier = 1;
       bp++;
